/*!
    \page ascent-fairness.html
    \title Provably Fair Randomness
    \previouspage Project Setup and the Game Loop
    \nextpage The Round State Machine

    This chapter decides where each round pops - and, more importantly, proves
    afterwards that the decision was not influenced by the player's bet.

    \section1 Why a Random Number Is Not Enough

    The obvious implementation is one line: draw a random number when the round
    starts and pop there. It works, and it is impossible to defend.

    Not because the number is predictable, but because nobody outside the code
    can tell when it was drawn. A game that decides the crash point after seeing
    a large bet looks exactly like a game that decided it before, and the player
    has no way to tell the two apart. In a game about nerve, "trust me" is a
    weak answer to a round that popped at 1.01x right after a big stake.

    So the game commits first and reveals later.

    \section1 Commit and Reveal

    Before betting opens, a secret seed is drawn and only its hash is published:

    \snippet src/ProvablyFair.cpp commitment

    A plain SHA-256, deliberately. The player can paste the seed into any hashing
    tool afterwards and get the same string - no custom scheme to take on faith.

    The ordering is what makes the hash mean anything, so it is enforced where
    the round is set up rather than left as a convention:

    \snippet src/LocalRoundSource.cpp open-betting

    The seed exists and its hash is on screen before the game will accept a
    single point. After that the game is committed: changing the seed would
    change the hash the player already saw, and the seed itself gives nothing
    away - which is the entire job of a hash function.

    \section1 Turning a Seed into a Crash Point

    The seed still has to become a multiplier, and the shape of that mapping is
    what makes the game feel like a crash game:

    \snippet src/ProvablyFair.cpp crash-point

    Fifty-two bits are read off the digest and read as a number between zero and
    one. Fifty-two, because that is how much a double holds without losing a
    single step - the fraction is exact rather than nearly exact.

    Then \c {1/(1-x)} stretches that flat number into a long tail. Half the
    rounds end below 2x, a tenth pass 10x, and one in a hundred runs past 100x.
    That distribution is the game: if most rounds paid 5x, waiting would be free,
    and if none did, nobody would wait at all.

    The house edge is not a fee subtracted from the payout. It shifts the whole
    curve down slightly, which mostly shows up as rounds that pop instantly at
    1.00x - about one in fifty. It is the same one percent either way, but this
    way the player sees it happen instead of finding it in the small print.

    The result is floored to two decimals for a reason that costs money if you
    get it wrong: the multiplier is shown with two decimals, so it must be
    decided with two decimals. A payout must never be worth more than the number
    the player read on screen.

    \section1 Letting the Player Check the Round

    When the round is over, the seed is published and the whole thing can be
    recomputed from scratch:

    \snippet src/ProvablyFair.cpp verify

    Both halves have to match. The seed must hash to the commitment that was
    shown before betting, and it must produce the crash point the round actually
    ended on. A game that swapped the seed fails the first check; a game that
    kept the seed but ended the round early fails the second.

    In the running game the seed replaces the hash on screen the moment the
    balloon pops, so the proof is available without leaving the round. A dedicated
    verification screen for past rounds comes later in the tutorial.

    None of this needs a server, and none of it is expensive - two hashes per
    round. What it buys is a game that can answer the only question a crash game
    ever gets asked.

    \include includes/chapters.qdocinc chapters
*/
